INT. CLOCKMAKER'S SHOP - CANAL STREET - AFTERNOON

A narrow shop wedged between a bakery and a bridge. Every wall ticks. Hundreds of clocks, none of them agreeing. Light through the bow window falls in long yellow bars across the workbench, where a velvet cloth lies flat and empty.

MIREILLE DUVAL, 60s, black wool, gloves still on, sits very straight in the only good chair. She does not look at the clocks. She looks at the cloth.

MR. ABERNATHY SOLOMON, 70, solicitor, bow tie and a folder of papers, clears his throat and gets no answer from the room.

KASPAR DUVAL, 30s, her nephew, leans in the doorway to the back, arms folded, a gold watch on his wrist that is a little too new.

TEODORA "TEDDY" ILIC, 19, apprentice, ink on her thumbs, stands by the workbench and keeps wiping the same spot with a rag.

CONSTABLE ROSSITER, 40s, damp from the canal towpath, stands inside the door and holds his helmet in both hands like a collection plate.

SOLOMON
If everyone is seated.

KASPAR
No one is seated.

SOLOMON
If everyone is present, then.

A cuckoo clock, somewhere, strikes the wrong hour. Nobody reacts. The cuckoo goes in.

SOLOMON (CONT'D)
Mr. Duval left the shop and its contents to his wife.

MIREILLE
Yes.

SOLOMON
With the exception of one item.

Teddy's rag stops. Kaspar's jaw moves, as if chewing something small.

MIREILLE
The Brandt.

SOLOMON
The Brandt regulator. Walnut case. 1791. Described in the will as "bequeathed to the one who has kept it running."

KASPAR
That's me.

TEDDY
It isn't.

KASPAR
Excuse me?

TEDDY
It stopped on Friday.

Silence. A hundred clocks tick on.

ROSSITER
Stopped how, miss?

TEDDY
Someone opened the case. Took out the weights.

MIREILLE
Kaspar. Your hands.

KASPAR
What about them?

MIREILLE
They are very clean for a man who works at a bank.

Kaspar looks at his hands. He turns them over. He pushes them into his pockets.

SOLOMON
I must remind everyone that the bequest is conditional.

ROSSITER
Conditional on what?

SOLOMON
On the clock being found in working order.

MIREILLE
Then it is nobody's.

SOLOMON
Madam, that would be the simplest reading.

Mireille stands. Smooths her coat. She crosses to the workbench and lifts the edge of the velvet cloth with one gloved finger. Underneath is a small brass key.

She studies it. Slides it into her handbag.

TEDDY
That's the winding key.

MIREILLE
Yes. It was hanging round his neck when they found him.

ROSSITER
Madam, that is evidence.

MIREILLE
Then you may have it, Constable. After tea.

She walks to the door. Rossiter steps aside without being asked.

KASPAR
Aunt Mireille.

MIREILLE
(not turning)
Wind your own watch, Kaspar.

She leaves. The bell above the door rings once. Kaspar looks down at the gold watch. It has stopped.

Teddy puts the rag down. Takes her coat from the hook.

TEDDY
I'll bring the weights back tomorrow.

SOLOMON
You have them?

TEDDY
I did not say that.

She leaves too. The bell rings. Rossiter puts on his helmet.

ROSSITER
Mr. Solomon, I think I shall need to read that will myself.

SOLOMON
It is rather long.

ROSSITER
I have time.

Solomon opens his folder. Kaspar watches the cloth.
